orrect.\n\nWait, but just to ensure, perhaps compute 131 ×742 as (130 +1) ×742 =130×742 +1×742.\n\nCalculating 130×742: 100×742=74,200; 30×742=22,260. Adding those: 74,200 +22,260 =96,460. Then add 1×742=742. Total is 96,460 +742 =97,202. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe 742 ×131. Let me try that way:\n\n742 ×100=74,200.\n\n742 ×30=22,260.\n\n742 ×1=742.\n\nAdding those: 74,200+22,260 is 96,460 +742=97,202. Same result. Okay, so that's consistent.\n\nTherefore, the product of 131 and 742 is indeed 97,202.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 131 and 742 is \\boxed{97202}.\n\n\nTo find the product of 131 and 742, we break down the multiplication step by step using the place values of each digit in 742.\n\n1. **Multiply 131 by the ones place digit (2):**\n   \\[\n   131 \\times 2 = 262\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 131 by the tens place digit (40):**\n   \\[\n   131 \\times 4 = 524 \\quad \\text{(then multiply by 10)} \\quad 524 \\times 10 = 5240\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 131 by the hundreds place digit (700):**\n   \\[\n   131 \\times 7 = 917 \\quad \\text{(then multiply by 100)} \\quad 917 \\times 100 = 91700\n   \\]\n\nNext, we sum the results of these multiplications:\n\\[\n262 + 5240 + 91700\n\\]\n\nFirst, add 262 and 5240:\n\\[\n262 + 5240 = 5502\n\\]\n\nThen, add the result to 91700:\n\\[\n5502 + 91700 = 97202\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 131 and 742 is \\(\\boxed{97202}\\).", '']
