e 263*400, which is 105,200, then 263*70=18,410, and 263*7=1,841. Then adding those: 105,200 +18,410 =123,610; plus 1,841 is 125,451. Wait, that's different approach but same result.\n\nWait, that's another way: 477 is 400 +70 +7. So 263*(400+70+7)=263*400 +263*70 +263*7.\n\n263*400: 263*4=1,052, so times 100 is 105,200.\n\n263*70: 263*7=1,841, so times 10 is 18,410.\n\n263*7=1,841.\n\nAdding those three:105,200 +18,410 =123,610. Then 123,610 +1,841 =125,451. Yep, same answer. So that confirms it.\n\nTherefore, the product is definitely 125,451. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in the steps. All methods lead to the same result. So I'm confident that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 477 and 263 is \\boxed{125451}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 477 and 263, we break it down step by step using the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. **Multiply 477 by the ones place digit (3) of 263:**\n   \\[\n   477 \\times 3 = 1431\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 477 by the tens place digit (60) of 263:**\n   \\[\n   477 \\times 60 = 477 \\times 6 \\times 10 = 2862 \\times 10 = 28620\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 477 by the hundreds place digit (200) of 263:**\n   \\[\n   477 \\times 200 = 477 \\times 2 \\times 100 = 954 \\times 100 = 95400\n   \\]\n\nNext, we sum all the partial products:\n\\[\n1431 + 28620 + 95400\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(1431\\) and \\(28620\\):\n\\[\n1431 + 28620 = 30051\n\\]\n\nThen, add the result to \\(95400\\):\n\\[\n30051 + 95400 = 125451\n\\]\n\nThus, the final computed product is \\(\\boxed{125451}\\).", '']
