te 828*600 = 496,800. Then 828*12 = 9,936. So total is 496,800 + 9,936. Let me add 496,800 + 9,936:\n\n496,800 + 9,000 = 505,800. Then add 936: 505,800 + 936 = 506,736. Yes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do 828*612 as (800+28)*(600+12). But that would be more complicated. But let me try:\n\nFirst, expand (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd.\n\nHere, a=800, b=28; c=600, d=12.\n\nSo:\n\nac = 800*600 = 480,000\n\nad = 800*12 = 9,600\n\nbc = 28*600 = 16,800\n\nbd = 28*12 = 336\n\nAdding all four: 480,000 +9,600 = 489,600; 489,600 +16,800 = 506,400; 506,400 +336 = 506,736. Yep, same answer again. So that's consistent.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct product is 506,736. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations now. All methods cross-verify to the same result. So I can be confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 828 and 612 is \\boxed{506736}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 828 and 612, we break down the multiplication by place value:\n\n1. Multiply 828 by the ones place digit (2) of 612:\n   \\[\n   828 \\times 2 = 1656\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 828 by the tens place digit (10) of 612:\n   \\[\n   828 \\times 10 = 8280\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 828 by the hundreds place digit (600) of 612:\n   \\[\n   828 \\times 600 = 496800\n   \\]\n\nNext, we sum all the results from each step:\n\\[\n1656 + 8280 + 496800\n\\]\n\nFirst, add the results of the ones and tens place multiplications:\n\\[\n1656 + 8280 = 9936\n\\]\n\nThen, add this result to the hundreds place multiplication result:\n\\[\n9936 + 496800 = 506736\n\\]\n\nThus, the final computed product of 828 and 612 is \\(\\boxed{506736}\\).",
