final_output:  ["153,440. Wait, let me add that again to be sure. 137,000 plus 16,440. 137,000 + 10,000 is 147,000, then plus 6,440 would be 153,440. Yep, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should do it another way to verify. Let me try multiplying 274 × 560 directly. \n\nAlternatively, 560 is 56 × 10, so maybe 274 × 56 first, then multiply by 10. Let's see if that gives the same result. \n\nCalculating 274 × 56. Breaking that down: 274 × 50 and 274 × 6. \n\n274 × 50: 274 × 5 = 1,370, so times 10 is 13,700. \n\n274 × 6 is 1,644 as before. Adding those gives 13,700 + 1,644 = 15,344. Then multiply by 10 (since 560 is 56×10) gives 153,440. Same result. Good, that cross-check works.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the distributive property: (200 + 70 + 4) × (500 + 60). Wait, that might complicate, but let's see. \n\nExpanding (200 +70 +4)*(500 +60) = 200*500 + 200*60 +70*500 +70*60 +4*500 +4*60. \n\nCalculating each term:\n\n200*500 = 100,000\n\n200*60 = 12,000\n\n70*500 = 35,000\n\n70*60 = 4,200\n\n4*500 = 2,000\n\n4*60 = 240\n\nAdding all these: \n\n100,000 + 12,000 = 112,000\n\n112,000 +35,000 = 147,000\n\n147,000 +4,200 = 151,200\n\n151,200 +2,000 = 153,200\n\n153,200 +240 = 153,440. \n\nSame answer again. So that's good. So regardless of the method, it comes out to 153,440. So I think that's the correct product.\n\nJust to ensure, maybe do a rough estimate. 274 is close to 270, and 560 is 560. 270 × 560. Let's see, 270 × 500 = 135,000, and 270 ×60=16,200. Total would be 151,200. The actual answer is 153,440, which is a bit higher, which makes sense because 274 is 4 more than 270, so 4×560=2,240. Adding that to 151,200 gives 153,440. Exactly. So that checks out.\n\nTherefore, I'm confident the answer is 153,440.\n\n</think>", '']
