      "text": "131,575? Wait, wait, let me check again:\n\nWait, adding 20,775 and 110,800:\n\n20,775 + 110,800. Let's think of 20k +110k =130k, and 775 +800=1,575. So total is 130,000 +1,575 =131,575. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo the total sum of all partial products is 131,575. Therefore, the product of 277 and 475 is 131,575.\n\nWait, but let me cross-verify using another method to be sure. Maybe using the standard algorithm again, but in a different way.\n\nAlternatively, use the distributive property:\n\n277 \u00d7475 =277\u00d7(400 +70 +5) =277\u00d7400 +277\u00d770 +277\u00d75. Which is exactly what I did earlier. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe compute 277 \u00d7475 directly:\n\nLet me try another approach: 277 \u00d7475. Let's see, perhaps breaking into smaller parts.\n\nAlternatively, 277 \u00d7475 = (200 +70 +7) \u00d7 (400 +70 +5). But that might complicate. Alternatively, use calculator-like steps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made an error in the partial products. Let me recheck each step:\n\nFirst partial product: 277 \u00d75.\n\n277 \u00d75: 200\u00d75=1000, 70\u00d75=350, 7\u00d75=35. Sum: 1000+350=1350 +35=1385. That's correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 277 \u00d770. Since it's the tens place, so 7\u00d710. So 277\u00d77\u00d710. 277\u00d77: 200\u00d77=1400, 70\u00d77=490, 7\u00d77=49. 1400+490=1890+49=1939. \u00d710 gives 19,390. Correct.\n\nThird partial product: 277\u00d7400. 277\u00d74=1,108 (since 200\u00d74=800, 70\u00d74=280, 7\u00d74=28; 800+280=1080+28=1108). \u00d7100 gives 110,800. Correct.\n\nAdding all three: 1,385 +19,390 =20,775; then +110,800 gives 131,575. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute 277\u00d7475 as follows:\n\nFirst, note that 475 is 500 -25. So 277\u00d7(500 -25) =277\u00d7500 -277\u00d725.\n\nCompute 277\u00d7500 =138,500 (since 277\u00d75=1,385, so \u00d7100 is 138,500).\n\nThen 277\u00d725: 277\u00d725 is the same as 277\u00d7(20+5)=277\u00d720 +277\u00d75. 277\u00d720=5,540 and 277\u00d75=1,385. So total is 5,540 +1,385 =6,925.\n\nTherefore, 138,500 -6,925 =?\n\n138,500 minus 6,925: 138,500 -6,000 =132,500; then subtract 925 more: 132,500 -925 =131,575. Same result. So that confirms it.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method like the lattice method, but that might take longer. But since two different methods give the same answer, I can be confident.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is 131,575. So I think that's correct. Let me just check once more with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, compute 277 \u00d7475:\n\nLet me write it vertically:\n\n\u2003\u2003277  \n\u00d7\u00a0475  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n\nFirst multiply 277 by 5 (units place):\n\n277 \u00d75 =1,385.\n\nThen multiply 277 by 70 (tens place, so 7\u00d710):\n\n277 \u00d77 =1,939, then \u00d710 is 19,390. So write that shifted one position to the left.\n\nNext, multiply 277 by 400 (hundreds place, 4\u00d7100):\n\n277 \u00d74 =1,108, then \u00d7100 is 110,800. Shifted two positions left.\n\nAdding them:\n\n1,385  \n19,390  \n110,800  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014  \nTotal: 1,385 +19,390 =20,775; 20,775 +110,800 =131,575. Yep, same as before.\n\nAlright, seems solid. So the answer is 131,575.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 277 and 475 is \\boxed{131575}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 277 and 475, we break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Multiply 277 by the ones place digit (5) of 475:**\n   \\[\n   277 \\times 5 = 1385\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 277 by the tens place digit (70) of 475:**\n   \\[\n   277 \\times 70 = 277 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 1939 \\times 10 = 19390\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 277 by the hundreds place digit (400) of 475:**\n   \\[\n   277 \\times 400 = 277 \\times 4 \\times 100 = 1108 \\times 100 = 110800\n   \\]\n\nNext, we sum all the partial products:\n\\[\n1385 + 19390 + 110800\n\\]\n\nFirst, add the first two results:\n\\[\n1385 + 19390 = 20775\n\\]\n\nThen add the result to the third partial product:\n\\[\n20775 + 110800 = 131575\n\\]\n\nThus, the final computed product is \\(\\boxed{131575}\\).",
