 multiplying by the tens digit (2 in the tens place is 20), so 802 × 20. So 802 × 2 = 1604, then add a zero to make it 16040. Hmm, but maybe the step-by-step multiplication would write it as 1604 shifted one place, so the second line would be 1604 but with a zero at the end? Wait, actually, when you do it the standard way, you write it without the zero and then shift. Let me think again.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps breaking it down:.\n\nFirst partial product: 802 × 1 = 802.\n\nSecond partial product: 802 × 20 (since the 2 is in the tens place). So that's 802 × 2 × 10 = 1604 × 10 = 16040? Wait, but 802 ×20 is 16,040? Let me confirm that. 800×20 is 16,000 and 2×20 is 40, so total 16,040. Yes, that's right.\n\nThird partial product: 802 × 300 (since the 3 is in the hundreds place). So 802 × 300. 800×300=240,000 and 2×300=600, so total 240,600.\n\nNow, adding all three partial products together:.\n\nFirst: 802.\n\nSecond: 16,040.\n\nThird: 240,600.\n\nAdding them step by step:.\n\n802 + 16,040 = 16,842</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
