final_output:  ["17,780. Wait, let me confirm that addition again. 14,000 plus 3,000 is 17,000, then plus 780 would be 17,780. Okay, that seems right.\n\nWait, but let me verify the multiplication another way to be sure. Maybe using the standard algorithm to cross-check. Let's do 140 × 127. Writing it out:\n\n   140\n×127\n-------\nFirst, multiply 140 by 7: 980 (as before)\nThen multiply 140 by 20 (the tens place, which is 2 in the tens place, so ×20): 2800\nThen multiply 140 by 100 (the hundreds place): 14,000\nAdding them gives 980 + 2800 = 3780; 3780 +14,000 =17,780. So that's the same as before. \n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking it down differently. Let's see: 127 can be written as 100 + 20 +7, so distributing the multiplication over addition: 140×(100+20+7)=140×100 +140×20 +140×7, which is exactly the same as above. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another method like the distributive property or the lattice method, but I think the standard way is sufficient here. The answer seems correct. I don't see any errors in the steps. So the final product should be 17,780.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 140 and 127 is \\boxed{17780}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 140 and 127, we break down the multiplication step by step using the place values of the digits in 127:\n\n1. Multiply 140 by the ones place digit (7) of 127:\n   \\[\n   140 \\times 7 = 980\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 140 by the tens place digit (20) of 127:\n   \\[\n   140 \\times 20 = 2800\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 140 by the hundreds place digit (100) of 127:\n   \\[\n   140 \\times 100 = 14000\n   \\]\n\nNext, we sum the results of these three steps:\n\\[\n980 + 2800 + 14000 = 17780\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 140 and 127 is \\(\\boxed{17780}\\).", '']
