 zero for the tens place makes it 12810? Wait, no, wait. Wait, 70 is 7×10, so multiplying by 7 first gives 1281, then ×10 is 12810. Wait, but 183×70 should be 183×7×10. Let me check the calculation again. .\n\nWait, when I did 183 ×7, I think I might have messed up. Let me recalculate that:.\n\n183 ×7:.\nStarting from the right:.\n3×7 =21, write 1, carryover 2.\n8×7=56 +2=58. So write 8, carryover 5.\n1×7=7 +5=12. So the result is 1281. Yeah, that's correct. Then multiplying by 10 gives 12810. So that's the second part.\n\nThird part is the hundreds place digit, which is 3, but it's actually 300. So multiply 183 by 300. Again, maybe do 183×3 first, then add two zeros.\n\n183 ×3:.\n3×3=9, 8×3=24 (carryover 2), 1×3=3 +2=5. So 549. Then adding two zeros gives 54900. .\n\nNow I need to add all three parts together: 549 (from the ones), 12810 (from the tens), and 54900 (from the hundreds). .\n\nLet me add them step by step. First, add 549 and 12810. .\n\n549 + 12810: .\n\n549 + 12,810. Let's see, 12,810 + 500 is 13,310, then plus 49 is 13,359. .\n\nThen add that to 54,900. .\n\n13,359 + 54,900. .\n\nAdding 13,359 + 50,000 is 63,359</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
