final_output:  ["314,160. Wait, let me verify that addition again to be sure. 308,000 plus 6,000 is 314,000, then plus 160 gives 314,160. That seems correct.\n\nHmm, but just to make sure I didn't make a mistake in any of the steps. Let me try an alternative method. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\u2003\u2003440  \n× 714  \n—————  \nFirst multiply 440 by 4 (ones place): 440 ×4 = 1,760  \nThen multiply 440 by 1 (tens place, so actually 10): 440 ×10 = 4,400  \nThen multiply 440 by 7 (hundreds place, so 700): 440 ×700 = 308,000  \nAdding those together: 1,760 + 4,400 = 6,160; then 6,160 + 308,000 = 314,160. Same result. Okay, that checks out. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another way to cross-verify. Let's see, 440 × 700 is 308,000. Then 440 ×14 is 440×10 +440×4 = 4,400 +1,760 =6,160. Then adding those gives the same total. Yep, that works.\n\nI think that's solid. The final answer should be 314,160.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 440 and 714 is \\boxed{314160}.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 440 and 714?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 714 has three digits, so we can break the multiplication into three steps:\n1. Multiply 440 by the ones place digit (4) of 714:  \n   \\(440 \\times 4 = 1760\\).\n2. Multiply 440 by the tens place digit (10) of 714:  \n   \\(440 \\times 10 = 4400\\).\n3. Multiply 440 by the hundreds place digit (700) of 714:  \n   \\(440 \\times 700 = 308000\\).\n\nNow, sum all the step results:  \n\\(1760 + 4400 + 308000 = 314160\\).\n\nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{314160}.", '']
