(from 81693's ten-thousands place?) Wait, 81,693 has 8 in the ten thousands place?\n\nWait 81,693 is 8 ten thousands (80,000) +1 thousand (1,000) +6 hundreds (600) +9 tens (90) +3 units. So in the ten thousands place: 8, thousands place:1, etc.\n\nSo the ten-thousands place in 281,700 is the second digit from the left: 8 (since 281,700 is 2 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), etc).\n\nSo the ten-thousands place addition:\n\n8 (from 281,700) + 8 (from 81,693) = 16. But wait, the 81,693's ten thousands digit is 8, and 281,700's ten thousands is 8? Wait no, 281,700 is 2 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), so 200,000 +80,000=280,000. So adding to the 8 (ten thousands) from 81,693:\n\nWait actually, when adding, the ten thousands place of 281,700 is 8, and the ten thousands place of 81,693 is 8. So adding those gives 16, which would be 6 in the ten thousands place with a carryover of 1 to the hundred thousands place.\n\nWait, let me do it step by step:\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a02 8 1 7 0 0\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0+\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a08 1 6 9 3\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nUnits place: 0 +3=3\n\nTens place:0 +9=9\n\nHundreds place:7 +6=13. Write down 3, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:1 (from first number) +1 (carryover) +1 (from second number's thousands place)= 1+1+1=3. Wait, the second number's thousands place is the fourth digit from the right, which in 81693 is the 1. Yes.\n\nTen-thousands place:8 (from first number) +8 (from second number's ten-thousands place)= 16. Wait, but the second number is 81,693 which has 8 in the ten thousands place. The first number has 8 in the ten thousands place (since it's 281,700). So 8 +8 =16. So write 6 in the ten-thousands place, carryover 1 to the hundred thousands place.\n\nHundred thousands place:2 (from first number) +0 (since second number has no hundred