he ones digit (5) \u2192 110\u00d75=550\n\n2. Multiply 110 by the tens digit (0, but as part of the tens place, so 0\u00d710, so 110\u00d70\u00d710? Wait no, in the example, step2 was 50 (the tens place digit is 5, so 5\u00d710=50, so they multiplied by 50). So similarly, here the tens digit is 0, so 0\u00d710=0, so step2 is 110\u00d70=0\n\n3. Multiply 110 by the hundreds digit (9\u00d7100=900) \u2192 110\u00d7900=99,000\n\nThen adding them: 550 + 0 + 99,000 = 99,550. So that's correct.\n\nSo the answer is indeed 99,550. I think that's solid. Let me just do a quick check with another method. 110\u00d7905. Since 110 is 100+10, so (100+10)\u00d7905 = 100\u00d7905 +10\u00d7905 = 90,500 + 9,050 = 99,550. Yep, matches. So all methods confirm the same result. I think that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 110 and 905 is \\boxed{99550}.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 110 and 905?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 905 has three digits, so we can break it down into three steps.\n1. Multiply 110 by the ones place digit 5 of 905:\n   \\[\n   110 \\times 5 = 550\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 110 by the tens place digit 0 (since the tens digit is 0) of 905:\n   \\[\n   110 \\times 0 = 0\n   \\]\n3. Multiply 110 by the hundreds place digit 900 (since the hundreds digit is 9) of 905:\n   \\[\n   110 \\times 900 = 99,\\!000\n   \\]\n\nNow, sum all the step results:\n\\[\n550 + 0 + 99,\\!000 = 99,\\!550\n\\]\n\nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{99550}.",
