926.\n\nYes, so that seems correct. I think the answer is 219,926.\n\nWait, but just to make sure I didn't make any arithmetic errors in the initial steps.\n\nLet me re-calculate 322 × 8:\n\n322 ×8:\n\n2×8=16, write down 6 carryover 1.\n\n2×8=16 +1=17, write down 7 carryover 1.\n\n3×8=24 +1=25. So 2576. That's correct. So 25760 when shifted.\n\n322 ×6:\n\n3×6=18, 2×6=12, 2×6=12. Wait, wait, 322 ×6:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n2×6=12, write 2 carryover 1.\n\nNext digit: 2×6=12 +1=13, write 3 carryover1.\n\n3×6=18 +1=19. So 1932. Correct. So 193200 when shifted by two zeros.\n\nSo adding all together again:\n\n966 (ones)\n\n25,760 (tens)\n\n193,200 (hundreds)\n\nTotal: 219,926. Okay, that's consistent.\n\nHmm, so I think that's the correct answer. I don't see any mistakes in the steps. So the product should be 219,926.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 322 and 683 is \\boxed{219926}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 322 and 683, we break down the multiplication step by step based on the digits of 683 (hundreds, tens, and ones places):\n\n1. **Multiply 322 by the ones place digit (3):**\n   \\[\n   322 \\times 3 = 966\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 322 by the tens place digit (80):**\n   \\[\n   322 \\times 80 = 322 \\times 8 \\times 10 = 2576 \\times 10 = 25760\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 322 by the hundreds place digit (600):**\n   \\[\n   322 \\times 600 = 322 \\times 6 \\times 100 = 1932 \\times 100 = 193200\n   \\]\n\nNext, we sum the results of these three steps:\n\\[\n966 + 25760 + 193200\n\\]\n\nFirst, add 966 and 25760:\n\\[\n966 + 25760 = 26726\n\\]\n\nThen add the result to 193200:\n\\[\n26726 + 193200 = 219926\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 322 and 683 is \\(\\boxed{219926}\\).", '']
