 90%|######### | 452/500 [5:44:50<30:12, 37.75s/it]["720,000 + 11,500 = 731,500? Wait, that doesn't add up right. Let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in adding. Let me recalculate:\n\n62,700 + 668,800. \n\nBreaking it down:\n\n62,700 + 668,800 = (62,700 + 600,000) + 68,800 = 662,700 + 68,800 = 731,500. Hmm, that's the same as before. But let me verify with another method.\n\nAlternatively, adding 62,700 + 668,800:\n\n62,700 + 668,800:\n\n62,700 + 668,800 = 668,800 + 62,700. \n\n668,800 + 60,000 is 728,800, then +2,700 is 731,500. So that's correct.\n\nWait, but let me cross-verify the total product using another method to ensure I didn't make an error in multiplication steps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the distributive property: 836 × 875 can be written as 836 × (800 + 70 +5) = 836×800 + 836×70 +836×5. Which is exactly what I did. So that's correct.\n\nBut let me check each partial product again.\n\nFirst partial product: 836 ×5 = 4,180. Correct.\n\nSecond: 836 ×70. Let me recalculate 836×7:\n\n800×7 = 5,600; 30×7=210; 6×7=42. Adding those: 5,600 + 210 = 5,810; +42 = 5,852. Then times 10 is 58,520. Correct.\n\nThird: 836×800. 836×8 is 6,688. Then ×100 is 668,800. Correct.\n\nAdding them: 4,180 +58,520 = 62,700. Then +668,800 = 731,500. Hmm, so that seems right. \n\nWait but I'm a bit confused because sometimes when numbers are large, it's easy to miscount zeros. Let me check with a calculator approach mentally.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute 836 × 875:\n\nFirst, note that 800×800 is 640,000, but that's not helpful. Alternatively, maybe break down 875 as 1000 - 125.\n\nWait, 875 = 1000 - 125. So 836 × (1000 - 125) = 836,000 - (836 ×125). \n\nHmm, maybe that's a longer route, but let's see:\n\nCompute 836 ×125 first. 125 is 100 +25. So 836×100=83,600; 836×25. \n\n836 ×25 = (800×25)+(36×25) = 20,000 + 900 = 20,900. \n\nThus, total 836×125 = 83,600 +20,900 = 104,500. \n\nTherefore, 836,000 - 104,500 = 731,500. So same result. So that confirms the answer is correct.\n\nTherefore, the final product is 731,500. I think that's correct. \n\nWait, but just to make sure I didn't make a mistake in the first calculation when adding the partial products. Let me add them again:\n\nFirst partial product: 4,180.\n\nSecond:58,520.\n\nThird:668,800.\n\nAdding all three:\n\nStart with 4,180 +58,520:\n\n4,180 +58,520 = 62,700.\n\nThen 62,700 +668,800:\n\n62,700 +668,800 = 731,500. Correct.\n\nAlright, so I think the answer is 731,500. \n\nJust to cross-verify once more with another method. Let's do 836 × 875.\n\nAlternatively, 836 × 875 = 836 × (800 +70 +5) = 836×800 +836×70 +836×5.\n\nWhich gives 668,800 + 58,520 +4,180. \n\nAdding 668,800 +58,520 first:\n\n668,800 +58,520 = 727,320. Then +4,180 gives 727,320 +4,180 = 731,500. Yep, same result.\n\nTherefore, confident that the answer is correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 836 and 875 is \\boxed{731500}.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 836 and 875?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 875 has three digits, so we can reason in three steps:\n1. Multiply 836 by the ones place digit 5 of 875:\n   \\[\n   836 \\times 5 = 4180\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 836 by the tens place digit 70 of 875:\n   \\[\n   836 \\times 70 = 58520\n   \\]\n3. Multiply 836 by the hundreds place digit 800 of 875:\n   \\[\n   836 \\times 800 = 668800\n   \\]\nAnswer:\nNow, sum all the step results:\n\\[\n4180 + 58520 + 668800 = 731500\n\\]\nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{731500}.", '']
